index 343f446..62740d8 100644
--- a/docs/4.examples/handle-session.md
+++ b/docs/4.examples/handle-session.md
@@ -42,6 +42,17 @@ app.use(async (event) => {
 >
 > The examples below use a hardcoded value so they stay readable, but in a real app load a randomly generated secret of at least 32 characters from an environment variable such as `process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD`, and never commit it to source control. A guessable passphrase (even one ≥32 characters) is not safe.
 
+> [!NOTE]
+> To rotate the password without signing users out, pass a list instead of a string. The first entry seals new sessions, and every entry is tried in order to open existing ones. Put the new password first and keep the old one until the sessions sealed with it have expired (`maxAge` or `idleTimeout`), then remove it. Removing an entry is the only way to revoke the sessions sealed with it, and each entry adds one unseal attempt for every cookie it does not open, so keep the list short.
+>
+> ```ts
+> useSession(event, {
+>   password: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW!, process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD!],
+> });
+> ```
+>
+> Some sessions keep the old password longer than you might expect: a session without `maxAge` or `idleTimeout` never expires, so it only moves to the new password when it is written. Sessions read from the `x-h3-session` header are never resealed, so they keep the old password until they expire.
+
 This will initialize a session and return an header `Set-Cookie` with a cookie named `h3` and an encrypted content.
 
 If the request contains a cookie named `h3` or a header named `x-h3-session`, the session will be initialized with the content of the cookie or the header.
